{
  "about": [
    "Cases for the Alpenglow floor on the version of an agave, jito or allnodes client and on the Yellowstone geyser_version of an RPC node.",
    "ansible/cmn/check_alpenglow_floor.yml (tests/rpc_geyser/test_alpenglow_floor.py) and cli/lib/versionFloor.ts both have to give these answers.",
    "A version is read as: an optional v; X.Y with an optional .Z (4.2 is 4.2.0); any further numeric components, which are ignored (4.2.1.1 is 4.2.1);",
    "an optional pre-release -alpha, -beta or -rc with an optional dot and number (a missing number is 0); then whatever follows, which is ignored",
    "(the jito and allnodes tags and any tag that is not known, so 3.1.10-bam is 3.1.10 and 4.3.0-beta.3-bam is 4.3.0-beta.3).",
    "reads is [major, minor, patch, rank, number] with rank 0 for alpha, 1 for beta, 2 for rc and 3 for a release; null when there is no X.Y.",
    "below says, per network, whether the version reads older than the floor of that network; a version that reads null is below nothing.",
    "A Yellowstone tag is read by the solana release it is built for: what follows +solana. up to the next + or space, with 4.4.0.beta.0 turned into 4.4.0-beta.0 (built_for is null when the tag has no +solana.).",
    "A client version that reads null cannot be compared: a pin like that is refused in a mainnet section and only warned about in the others (the Ansible check stops the run on mainnet and says it elsewhere).",
    "A Yellowstone tag that has no +solana. part, or whose part reads null, is not checked.",
    "An empty version is no version and is not a case here: the Ansible check and the builders stop on it whatever the network. An empty Yellowstone tag is not a case either (the Ansible check takes the pin of the section then)."
  ],
